    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Centreville was founded in 1782 when the county seat moved from Queenstown to a more central spot on the Corsica River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/centreville_historic_district_centreville_maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1871 the Queen Anne's and Kent Railroad turned Centreville into a railroad town, ending a line that stretched to Townsend, Delaware.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centreville%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s historic core follows the Corsica River, Gravel Run, and Mill Stream, shaping its irregular boundary of 400 historic resources.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/centreville_historic_district_centreville_maryland",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Centreville’s name reflects both its central location in Queen Anne’s County and its position on the Corsica River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centreville%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Queen Anne's County Fair, held each summer, is a long‑standing tradition that draws visitors to Centreville’s historic square.",
